{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LScM2KCdAqdiLicxSkF4Dj","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Domain Requirements: Diagram Representations\n     2→\n     3→> **Status (2026-02-15):** All core functionality delivered. Schema cleanup, arch directory, mermaid rendering, migration/cleanup all complete. Chat review panel superseded by architecture-chat v2 (protocol-native streaming). Draw.io embedded viewer not yet implemented.\n     4→\n     5→## Problem Statement\n     6→\n     7→Vista currently stores all architecture diagrams (UI flows, data models, sequence diagrams, services, implementation phases) as structured JSON inside a monolithic `_plan.json` file, then renders them as custom SVG via vanilla JavaScript. This approach has three problems:\n     8→\n     9→1. **No native editing** - Diagrams can't be opened in standard tools (Mermaid live editor, Draw.io). Editing requires modifying JSON and re-rendering.\n    10→2. **Rendering complexity** - 1,500+ lines of custom SVG layout code in template.html that must be maintained for every diagram type.\n    11→3. **Review friction** - The current approve/reject-per-item flow is passive. Users need an interactive way to review diagrams with AI assistance.\n    12→\n    13→## Users & Personas\n    14→\n    15→- **Developer (primary)** - Uses Vista to plan features. Wants to see architecture diagrams, edit them in native tools, and discuss them with AI before approving.\n    16→- **Plugin author** - Creates Vista planning workflows. Needs a simple file-based format that agents can generate (Mermaid syntax is LLM-friendly).\n    17→\n    18→## Business Objectives\n    19→\n    20→1. Replace the monolithic plan JSON diagram sections with native file formats (.mmd, .drawio)\n    21→2. Simplify the rendering pipeline by using established libraries (mermaid.js, drawio viewer)\n    22→3. Replace the passive review flow with an interactive AI chat interface\n    23→4. Keep the plan lightweight - `_plan.json` references the arch/ directory rather than embedding diagram data\n    24→\n    25→## Success Metrics\n    26→\n    27→- All 5 current diagram types representable via .mmd or .drawio files\n    28→- Web app renders diagrams from native files with zero custom SVG code\n    29→- AI chat interface connects to user's CLI providers (Claude, OpenCode) for review\n    30→- Agents can generate .mmd files directly (no intermediate JSON translation)\n    31→\n    32→## Functional Requirements\n    33→\n    34→### Core Functionality\n    35→\n    36→1. **Architecture directory** - Each feature gets an `arch/` subdirectory containing native diagram files and a `_arch.json` manifest\n    37→2. **Manifest file** - `_arch.json` registers each diagram with name, file reference, type (mermaid/drawio), diagram subtype, and description\n    38→3. **Mermaid rendering** - Web app renders .mmd files client-side via mermaid.js\n    39→4. **Draw.io rendering** - Web app renders .drawio files via embedded viewer/iframe\n    40→5. **Plan reference** - `_plan.json` includes an `architecture.ref` field pointing to `./arch/_arch.json`\n    41→6. **AI chat panel** - Side-by-side chat interface that slides out from a button (Notion-style popup)\n    42→7. **Provider/model selection** - Chat dropdown selects from user's enabled CLI providers and their models\n    43→8. **Diagram context injection** - User clicks a diagram to add its content to the AI chat context\n    44→\n    45→### User Workflows\n    46→\n    47→1. **Plan generation** - Agent generates .mmd files in arch/ and writes _arch.json manifest\n    48→2. **Diagram viewing** - User opens web app, sees rendered diagrams organized by manifest\n    49→3. **AI review** - User clicks chat button, selects provider/model, clicks diagram to add context, discusses changes\n    50→4. **Diagram editing** - User edits .mmd files directly in text editor or via AI suggestions\n    51→\n    52→### Business Rules\n    53→\n    54→- _arch.json is the single source of truth for which diagrams exist\n    55→- Diagram files are stored in their native format - no format translation\n    56→- The web app reads the manifest first, then loads each file by type\n    57→- Chat messages are routed via protocol-native streaming (Claude WebSocket, OpenCode SSE) through ProviderRouter\n    58→\n    59→## Non-Functional Requirements\n    60→\n    61→- **Performance:** Mermaid diagrams render client-side in < 1s. Draw.io viewer loads via iframe.\n    62→- **Platform:** Web only (Vista server dashboard)\n    63→- **Offline:** Not required - server must be running\n    64→- **Security:** Provider connections run server-side with user's existing permissions. Chat sessions persist to disk with project-scoped isolation.\n    65→- **Accessibility:** Diagrams should have alt text from manifest descriptions\n    66→\n    67→## Constraints & Dependencies\n    68→\n    69→- **Technical:** mermaid.js loaded from CDN or bundled. Draw.io viewer requires iframe embedding.\n    70→- **Dependencies:** ProviderRouter for chat (routes to Claude via Companion WebSocket, OpenCode via HTTP SSE). Existing FastAPI server for routes. Chat sessions persisted via ChatSessionService.\n    71→- **Breaking change:** Replaces the current plan JSON sections (uiFlows, dataModels, dataFlow, services) and removes the approve/reject review system entirely.\n    72→\n    73→## User Experience Requirements\n    74→\n    75→- **Discovery:** Diagrams tab/section on the feature page in the web dashboard\n    76→- **Journey:** Open feature -> see rendered diagrams -> click chat button -> select provider -> click diagram for context -> discuss with AI\n    77→- **Feedback:** Loading spinners while diagrams render. Chat shows streaming responses.\n    78→- **Error handling:** Missing .mmd file shows placeholder. Provider connection errors shown in chat.\n    79→\n    80→---\n    81→\n    82→## Expansion: Multi-Format Diagram Support & Markdown Editing\n    83→\n    84→*Added 2026-02-15*\n    85→\n    86→### Problem Statement (Expansion)\n    87→\n    88→The original feature scope covers Mermaid (.mmd) and Draw.io (.drawio) formats. In practice, different diagram types are better suited to different tools — PlantUML excels at UML class/sequence diagrams, D2 provides modern declarative architecture diagrams with auto-layout, and Graphviz is the standard for dependency graphs and network topologies. Additionally, architecture documentation often includes prose alongside diagrams, requiring native Markdown rendering and inline editing.\n    89→\n    90→### New Functional Requirements\n    91→\n    92→#### Multi-Format Rendering\n    93→\n    94→5. **PlantUML rendering (.puml)** — Encode diagrams client-side via `plantuml-encoder`, render SVG by fetching from a PlantUML server (self-hosted or plantuml.com). The server URL is configurable in Vista settings.\n    95→6. **D2 rendering (.d2)** — Render client-side via `@terrastruct/d2` WASM package using dagre or elk layout engines. TALA layout (server-dependent) is not supported.\n    96→7. **Graphviz rendering (.dot)** — Render client-side via `@viz-js/viz` WASM package. Supports all Graphviz layout engines (dot, neato, fdp, sfdp, circo, twopi, osage, patchwork).\n    97→8. **Markdown rendering (.md)** — Render Markdown files client-side via a markdown library (e.g. `marked`, `markdown-it`, or `react-markdown`). Supports GFM (GitHub Flavored Markdown) including tables, task lists, and fenced code blocks.\n    98→\n    99→#### Markdown Inline Editing\n   100→\n   101→9. **Edit button** — Each rendered Markdown card displays a small pencil/edit icon button in the top-right corner.\n   102→10. **Edit mode** — Clicking the edit button switches the card from rendered view to a raw Markdown text editor (textarea or CodeMirror-style editor).\n   103→11. **Save/cancel** — Edit mode shows save (checkmark) and cancel (X) buttons. Save writes the updated content back to the .md file on disk via the server API. Cancel discards changes and returns to rendered view.\n   104→12. **Auto-save indicator** — When saving, a brief loading indicator shows, then confirms success or displays an error.\n   105→\n   106→#### Manifest & Type System Updates\n   107→\n   108→13. **Extended type enum** — `_arch.json` manifest `type` field expands from `mermaid | drawio` to `mermaid | drawio | plantuml | d2 | graphviz | markdown`.\n   109→14. **Renderer dispatch** — The web app reads the `type` field from each manifest entry and dispatches to the appropriate renderer component.\n   110→15. **File extension mapping** — `.mmd` → mermaid, `.drawio` → drawio, `.puml` → plantuml, `.d2` → d2, `.dot` → graphviz, `.md` → markdown.\n   111→\n   112→### Updated User Workflows\n   113→\n   114→5. **Plan generation (expanded)** — Agent can generate diagrams in any supported format. Mermaid remains the default for LLM-generated diagrams; other formats are used when specifically appropriate (e.g., `.dot` for dependency graphs, `.puml` for detailed UML).\n   115→6. **Markdown documentation** — Agent generates .md files in arch/ for prose documentation (design rationale, ADRs, component descriptions). These render alongside diagrams.\n   116→7. **Inline markdown editing** — User clicks edit button on a markdown card, modifies content in-place, and saves back to disk without leaving the dashboard.\n   117→\n   118→### Updated Business Rules\n   119→\n   120→- Renderer selection is driven by the `type` field in `_arch.json`, not by file extension (though the two should be consistent).\n   121→- PlantUML rendering depends on network access to a PlantUML server. If the server is unreachable, show an error with the raw source as fallback.\n   122→- D2 rendering uses only client-side layouts (dagre/elk). TALA layout is explicitly unsupported.\n   123→- Markdown editing writes directly to the source .md file — there is no draft/version system.\n   124→\n   125→### Updated Non-Functional Requirements\n   126→\n   127→- **Performance:** Graphviz and D2 WASM modules should load lazily (only when a diagram of that type appears). PlantUML server calls should complete in < 2s.\n   128→- **Bundle size:** WASM renderers (D2, Graphviz) are loaded on-demand, not included in the main bundle. Total additional payload per renderer: ~1-3 MB.\n   129→- **Offline:** PlantUML rendering requires network access to the configured PlantUML server. All other formats work offline.\n   130→\n   131→### Updated Constraints & Dependencies\n   132→\n   133→- **New npm packages:** `@terrastruct/d2`, `@viz-js/viz`, `plantuml-encoder`, plus a markdown renderer\n   134→- **PlantUML server:** Either plantuml.com (default) or a self-hosted instance. URL stored in `~/.vista/settings.json` as `plantuml_server_url`.\n   135→- **Server API addition:** `PUT /api/projects/{id}/features/{name}/arch/{filename}` endpoint for saving edited markdown files.\n   136→\n   137→### New TDD Candidates\n   138→\n   139→- **RendererDispatch** — Logic for selecting the correct renderer based on manifest type. Multiple code paths with edge cases (unknown type, missing renderer library).\n   140→- **PlantUMLEncoder** — Encoding diagrams and constructing server URLs. Needs tests for encoding correctness and error handling when server is unreachable.\n   141→- **MarkdownEditor** — State machine for view/edit/saving modes. Needs tests for save success, save failure, cancel without saving, and unsaved changes warning.\n   142→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e23a7aa2-5923-4855-9cc2-f80458cf6b25","uuid":"08fc01eb-6cbe-41a2-b219-e62f93fe8911","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Grove/OneDrive/Documents/PYTHON/claude_plugins/vista-expanded-visualization/.vista/features/diagram_representations/domain-requirements.md","content":"# Domain Requirements: Diagram Representations\n\n> **Status (2026-02-15):** All core functionality delivered. Schema cleanup, arch directory, mermaid rendering, migration/cleanup all complete. Chat review panel superseded by architecture-chat v2 (protocol-native streaming). Draw.io embedded viewer not yet implemented.\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nVista currently stores all architecture diagrams (UI flows, data models, sequence diagrams, services, implementation phases) as structured JSON inside a monolithic `_plan.json` file, then renders them as custom SVG via vanilla JavaScript. This approach has three problems:\n\n1. **No native editing** - Diagrams can't be opened in standard tools (Mermaid live editor, Draw.io). Editing requires modifying JSON and re-rendering.\n2. **Rendering complexity** - 1,500+ lines of custom SVG layout code in template.html that must be maintained for every diagram type.\n3. **Review friction** - The current approve/reject-per-item flow is passive. Users need an interactive way to review diagrams with AI assistance.\n\n## Users & Personas\n\n- **Developer (primary)** - Uses Vista to plan features. Wants to see architecture diagrams, edit them in native tools, and discuss them with AI before approving.\n- **Plugin author** - Creates Vista planning workflows. Needs a simple file-based format that agents can generate (Mermaid syntax is LLM-friendly).\n\n## Business Objectives\n\n1. Replace the monolithic plan JSON diagram sections with native file formats (.mmd, .drawio)\n2. Simplify the rendering pipeline by using established libraries (mermaid.js, drawio viewer)\n3. Replace the passive review flow with an interactive AI chat interface\n4. Keep the plan lightweight - `_plan.json` references the arch/ directory rather than embedding diagram data\n\n## Success Metrics\n\n- All 5 current diagram types representable via .mmd or .drawio files\n- Web app renders diagrams from native files with zero custom SVG code\n- AI chat interface connects to user's CLI providers (Claude, OpenCode) for review\n- Agents can generate .mmd files directly (no intermediate JSON translation)\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n### Core Functionality\n\n1. **Architecture directory** - Each feature gets an `arch/` subdirectory containing native diagram files and a `_arch.json` manifest\n2. **Manifest file** - `_arch.json` registers each diagram with name, file reference, type (mermaid/drawio), diagram subtype, and description\n3. **Mermaid rendering** - Web app renders .mmd files client-side via mermaid.js\n4. **Draw.io rendering** - Web app renders .drawio files via embedded viewer/iframe\n5. **Plan reference** - `_plan.json` includes an `architecture.ref` field pointing to `./arch/_arch.json`\n6. **AI chat panel** - Side-by-side chat interface that slides out from a button (Notion-style popup)\n7. **Provider/model selection** - Chat dropdown selects from user's enabled CLI providers and their models\n8. **Diagram context injection** - User clicks a diagram to add its content to the AI chat context\n\n### User Workflows\n\n1. **Plan generation** - Agent generates .mmd files in arch/ and writes _arch.json manifest\n2. **Diagram viewing** - User opens web app, sees rendered diagrams organized by manifest\n3. **AI review** - User clicks chat button, selects provider/model, clicks diagram to add context, discusses changes\n4. **Diagram editing** - User edits .mmd files directly in text editor or via AI suggestions\n\n### Business Rules\n\n- _arch.json is the single source of truth for which diagrams exist\n- Diagram files are stored in their native format - no format translation\n- The web app reads the manifest first, then loads each file by type\n- Chat messages are routed via protocol-native streaming (Claude WebSocket, OpenCode SSE) through ProviderRouter\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Performance:** Mermaid diagrams render client-side in < 1s. Draw.io viewer loads via iframe.\n- **Platform:** Web only (Vista server dashboard)\n- **Offline:** Not required - server must be running\n- **Security:** Provider connections run server-side with user's existing permissions. Chat sessions persist to disk with project-scoped isolation.\n- **Accessibility:** Diagrams should have alt text from manifest descriptions\n\n## Constraints & Dependencies\n\n- **Technical:** mermaid.js loaded from CDN or bundled. Draw.io viewer requires iframe embedding.\n- **Dependencies:** ProviderRouter for chat (routes to Claude via Companion WebSocket, OpenCode via HTTP SSE). Existing FastAPI server for routes. Chat sessions persisted via ChatSessionService.\n- **Breaking change:** Replaces the current plan JSON sections (uiFlows, dataModels, dataFlow, services) and removes the approve/reject review system entirely.\n\n## User Experience Requirements\n\n- **Discovery:** Diagrams tab/section on the feature page in the web dashboard\n- **Journey:** Open feature -> see rendered diagrams -> click chat button -> select provider -> click diagram for context -> discuss with AI\n- **Feedback:** Loading spinners while diagrams render. Chat shows streaming responses.\n- **Error handling:** Missing .mmd file shows placeholder. Provider connection errors shown in chat.\n\n---\n\n## Expansion: Multi-Format Diagram Support & Markdown Editing\n\n*Added 2026-02-15*\n\n### Problem Statement (Expansion)\n\nThe original feature scope covers Mermaid (.mmd) and Draw.io (.drawio) formats. In practice, different diagram types are better suited to different tools — PlantUML excels at UML class/sequence diagrams, D2 provides modern declarative architecture diagrams with auto-layout, and Graphviz is the standard for dependency graphs and network topologies. Additionally, architecture documentation often includes prose alongside diagrams, requiring native Markdown rendering and inline editing.\n\n### New Functional Requirements\n\n#### Multi-Format Rendering\n\n5. **PlantUML rendering (.puml)** — Encode diagrams client-side via `plantuml-encoder`, render SVG by fetching from a PlantUML server (self-hosted or plantuml.com). The server URL is configurable in Vista settings.\n6. **D2 rendering (.d2)** — Render client-side via `@terrastruct/d2` WASM package using dagre or elk layout engines. TALA layout (server-dependent) is not supported.\n7. **Graphviz rendering (.dot)** — Render client-side via `@viz-js/viz` WASM package. Supports all Graphviz layout engines (dot, neato, fdp, sfdp, circo, twopi, osage, patchwork).\n8. **Markdown rendering (.md)** — Render Markdown files client-side via a markdown library (e.g. `marked`, `markdown-it`, or `react-markdown`). Supports GFM (GitHub Flavored Markdown) including tables, task lists, and fenced code blocks.\n\n#### Markdown Inline Editing\n\n9. **Edit button** — Each rendered Markdown card displays a small pencil/edit icon button in the top-right corner.\n10. **Edit mode** — Clicking the edit button switches the card from rendered view to a raw Markdown text editor (textarea or CodeMirror-style editor).\n11. **Save/cancel** — Edit mode shows save (checkmark) and cancel (X) buttons. Save writes the updated content back to the .md file on disk via the server API. Cancel discards changes and returns to rendered view.\n12. **Auto-save indicator** — When saving, a brief loading indicator shows, then confirms success or displays an error.\n\n#### Manifest & Type System Updates\n\n13. **Extended type enum** — `_arch.json` manifest `type` field expands from `mermaid | drawio` to `mermaid | drawio | plantuml | d2 | graphviz | markdown`.\n14. **Renderer dispatch** — The web app reads the `type` field from each manifest entry and dispatches to the appropriate renderer component.\n15. **File extension mapping** — `.mmd` → mermaid, `.drawio` → drawio, `.puml` → plantuml, `.d2` → d2, `.dot` → graphviz, `.md` → markdown.\n\n### Updated User Workflows\n\n5. **Plan generation (expanded)** — Agent can generate diagrams in any supported format. Mermaid remains the default for LLM-generated diagrams; other formats are used when specifically appropriate (e.g., `.dot` for dependency graphs, `.puml` for detailed UML).\n6. **Markdown documentation** — Agent generates .md files in arch/ for prose documentation (design rationale, ADRs, component descriptions). These render alongside diagrams.\n7. **Inline markdown editing** — User clicks edit button on a markdown card, modifies content in-place, and saves back to disk without leaving the dashboard.\n\n### Updated Business Rules\n\n- Renderer selection is driven by the `type` field in `_arch.json`, not by file extension (though the two should be consistent).\n- PlantUML rendering depends on network access to a PlantUML server. If the server is unreachable, show an error with the raw source as fallback.\n- D2 rendering uses only client-side layouts (dagre/elk). TALA layout is explicitly unsupported.\n- Markdown editing writes directly to the source .md file — there is no draft/version system.\n\n### Updated Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Performance:** Graphviz and D2 WASM modules should load lazily (only when a diagram of that type appears). PlantUML server calls should complete in < 2s.\n- **Bundle size:** WASM renderers (D2, Graphviz) are loaded on-demand, not included in the main bundle. Total additional payload per renderer: ~1-3 MB.\n- **Offline:** PlantUML rendering requires network access to the configured PlantUML server. All other formats work offline.\n\n### Updated Constraints & Dependencies\n\n- **New npm packages:** `@terrastruct/d2`, `@viz-js/viz`, `plantuml-encoder`, plus a markdown renderer\n- **PlantUML server:** Either plantuml.com (default) or a self-hosted instance. URL stored in `~/.vista/settings.json` as `plantuml_server_url`.\n- **Server API addition:** `PUT /api/projects/{id}/features/{name}/arch/{filename}` endpoint for saving edited markdown files.\n\n### New TDD Candidates\n\n- **RendererDispatch** — Logic for selecting the correct renderer based on manifest type. Multiple code paths with edge cases (unknown type, missing renderer library).\n- **PlantUMLEncoder** — Encoding diagrams and constructing server URLs. Needs tests for encoding correctness and error handling when server is unreachable.\n- **MarkdownEditor** — State machine for view/edit/saving modes. Needs tests for save success, save failure, cancel without saving, and unsaved changes warning.\n","numLines":142,"startLine":1,"totalLines":142}}}
